rtunity to change this the  idea was simple he would launch a new  cryptocurrency and then integrate it  directly into facebook this new project  would be called libra which isn't quite  as catchy as zuckerbox but there was a  lot at stake so i get why they wanted to  use a more professional name the first  question that came up immediately was  why not just use bitcoin tens of  millions of people were already using  bitcoin but the network was too slow for  zuckerberg's vision bitcoin could only  handle around seven transactions per  second but libra would scale to  thousands of transactions per second  sounds good in theory but when you're  dealing with crypto everything is about  trade-offs bitcoin has attracted so many  devotees precisely because there is no  need to trust anyone you can verify the  entire blockchain yourself if you like  but this comes at the cost of efficiency  of transactions at the opposite end of  this spectrum would be a coin completely  controlled by facebook you'd have to  trust them absolutely but they could  just store all of the transactions in a  centralized database and maximize  transaction speed now zuckerberg and his  team realized early on that this would  be a non-starter people were having a  hard enough time trusting facebook with  pictures of their cats so giving  zuckerberg complete control over their  money would be a stretch to say the  least so the libra team came up with a  compromise they would build something  that sat halfway between the fully  decentralized bitcoin blockchain and a  centralized database like visa this  distributed ledger structure would allow  libra to be the best of both worlds at  least in theory instead of facebook  having total control over the network 28  separate organizations would work  together to validate transactions these  validators were all big companies like  paypal and mastercard and they each put  up 10 million dollars and promised to  provide computing power on an ongoing  basis th